1
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
One adventure will change two worlds.

2
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
How to Train Your Dragon is a fantasy, adventure,
animation film directed by Dean DeBlois.

3
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:36,000
As the son of a Viking leader on the cusp of manhood, shy Hiccup Horrendous Haddock III
faces a rite of passage: he must kill a dragon to prove his warrior mettle.

4
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
But after downing a feared dragon, he realizes that he no longer wants to destroy it, and instead
befriends the beast – which he names Toothless – much to the chagrin of his warrior father.

5
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Hiccup (voice), played by Jay Baruchel.

6
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:06,000
Stoick (voice), played by Gerard Butler.

7
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Gobber (voice), played by Craig Ferguson.

8
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Astrid (voice), played by America Ferrera.

9
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Snotlout (voice), played by Jonah Hill.

10
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:06,000
Fishlegs (voice), played by Christopher Mintz-Plasse.

11
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:36,000
How to Train Your Dragon is a 2010 American animated fantasy film directed by Chris Sanders and Dean DeBlois
and written by Sanders, DeBlois and Will Davies, based on the 2003 novel by Cressida Cowell.

12
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Produced by DreamWorks Animation, the film stars the voices of Jay Baruchel, Gerard Butler, Craig
Ferguson, America Ferrera, Jonah Hill, Christopher Mintz-Plasse, Kristen Wiig and T.J.

13
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
The story takes place in Berk, a mythical Viking village.

14
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:06,000
Hiccup, an undersized teen outcast and son of the village chieftain, wishing to become a dragon slayer like
the other Vikings, injures a rare Night Fury dragon but is unable to bring himself to kill it.

15
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
He instead helps and befriends the dragon, and quickly discovers that things are not
exactly as they seem in the conflict between Vikings and dragons.

16
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
In 2004, the book series began attracting the attention
of executives at DreamWorks Animation.

17
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:36,000
After the success of Over the Hedge (2006), producer Bonnie Arnold
became interested in the newly acquired property.

18
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:06,000
The directors of the film wanted to ensure they took advantage of the improvisation abilities of
the secondary cast by frequently bringing them together in the recording sessions.

19
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:36,000
The filmmakers hired cinematographer Roger Deakins as a visual consultant to help
them with the aesthetics of the film and to add a live-action feel.

20
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
John Powell composed the film's musical score.

21
01:13:30,000 --> 01:13:36,000
How to Train Your Dragon premiered at the Gibson Amphitheater on March 21, 2010, and
was released in the United States on March 26 by Paramount Pictures.

22
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:06,000
The film was a commercial success, earning $495 million and
becoming the tenth-highest-grossing film of 2010.

23
01:20:30,000 --> 01:20:36,000
It received critical acclaim, with praise for its
animation, writing, and 3D sequences.

24
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
It received numerous accolades, including two Academy Award nominations, and spawned a a multimedia franchise, including
two sequels, How to Train Your Dragon 2 (2014) and How to Train Your Dragon: The Hidden World (2019).

25
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:36,000
A live-action remake was released in 2025.

26
01:31:00,000 --> 01:31:06,000
The film explores themes of friendship, ship,
blacksmith, island, based on novel or book.

27
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:36,000
The tone is lighthearted and uplifting.
